---

<Docs
  title="Stack vs Terraform"
  description="Terraform is the standard for cloud infrastructure. Stack provisions SaaS services. Different layers, often used together."
  eyebrow="Comparison"
  extraSchemas={[faq]}
>
  <h2>TL;DR</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform</a>
    is HashiCorp's battle-tested IaC tool: you write HCL that describes cloud
    primitives, and Terraform reconciles your described state against the
    cloud. It has the largest provider ecosystem in the industry (3,000+
    providers on the registry), mature state management, and is the default
    choice for anything touching AWS, GCP, or Azure. Stack connects
    supported SaaS services through provider-specific setup, with secrets
    routed through Phantom and MCP tooling. Stack is not a Terraform replacement. It's what
    you reach for when Terraform's provider coverage runs out.
  </p>

  <h2>When to pick each</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Pick Terraform when</th>
        <th>Pick Stack when</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>You own AWS/GCP/Azure accounts and need VPCs, IAM, RDS, Lambda, CloudFront, GKE.</td>
        <td>You rent SaaS: Supabase, Stripe, Clerk, Vercel, PostHog, Resend.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Multi-region, multi-account, remote state, workspaces, policy-as-code (Sentinel / OPA).</td>
        <td>You want <code>stack add stripe</code> to verify and store an existing restricted key in Phantom.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Regulated industry with audit trails and drift detection at the cloud-primitive layer.</td>
        <td>You want to compose supported SaaS services with explicit provider authorization.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The quickest decision heuristic: if the thing you're provisioning lives in
    the AWS, GCP, or Azure console, Terraform is the right tool. If it lives
    in the Supabase, Stripe, or Clerk dashboard, Stack is the right tool.
  </p>

  <h2>At-a-glance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Capability</th>
        <th>Stack</th>
        <th>Terraform</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Category</td>
        <td>SaaS control plane</td>
        <td>Cloud IaC</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Primary surface</td>
        <td>CLI + MCP server + Claude Code plugin</td>
        <td>CLI (HCL DSL); Terraform Cloud / Enterprise for collaboration</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Language</td>
        <td>Declarative TOML (<code>.stack.toml</code>)</td>
        <td>HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>What it provisions</td>
        <td>Provider-specific setup for Supabase, Vercel, Stripe, Clerk, PostHog, Sentry, GitHub, Resend, Neon, Fly.io, Railway, and others</td>
        <td>AWS, GCP, Azure, Kubernetes, Cloudflare, 3,000+ providers</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>State management</td>
        <td>Shape-file only (<code>.stack.toml</code>); Phantom holds values; no server</td>
        <td>Local or remote state file; locking via backend (S3 + DynamoDB, Terraform Cloud, etc.)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Secrets</td>
        <td>Credential values routed through <a href="/docs/phantom/">Phantom</a>; <code>.stack.toml</code> records slot names</td>
        <td>Variables marked <code>sensitive</code>; state file contains plaintext secrets unless encrypted at rest</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>AI surface</td>
        <td>
          <a href="/docs/recommend/">Native <code>stack recommend</code></a> + 19 MCP tools + Claude Code plugin
        </td>
        <td>HashiCorp AI in Terraform Cloud (preview); no native MCP</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>License</td>
        <td>MIT · pre-alpha v{PUBLISHED_VERSION}</td>
        <td>BSL 1.1 (since 2023); OpenTofu is the Apache 2.0 fork</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Free tier</td>
        <td>Entire CLI, MCP, plugin — free forever</td>
        <td>CLI free; Terraform Cloud free for up to 5 users</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Best for</td>
        <td>SaaS composition, indie devs, YC startups, agentic workflows</td>
        <td>Platform engineering, SRE, regulated cloud deployments</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where they overlap</h2>
  <p>
    The overlap is real but narrow. Terraform's provider registry includes
    community-maintained wrappers for a handful of SaaS products — there's a
    <code>supabase/supabase</code> provider, a <code>stripe/stripe</code>
    provider, a <code>cloudflare/cloudflare</code> provider that's actually
    first-class. In theory you can write HCL that creates a Stripe product,
    a Supabase branch, a Clerk application. In practice the ergonomics are
    wrong: you're spinning up a state backend, writing 80 lines of HCL, and
    handling credentials — while <code>stack add stripe</code> can verify
    and store an existing restricted key through Phantom.
  </p>
  <p>
    The honest cases where Terraform's SaaS providers are the right answer:
    you already have Terraform in production, your platform team owns all
    provisioning, and the SaaS resources need to live in the same state graph
    as your cloud resources.
  </p>

  <h2>Where Stack wins</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Provider-specific SaaS setup.</strong> <code>stack add supabase</code>
      uses OAuth or a supplied PAT, calls the Supabase Management API to
      create a project, fetches available service-role and anon keys, stores
      credentials in Phantom, records slot names in <code>.stack.toml</code>,
      and merges its MCP entry into <code>.mcp.json</code>. App environment
      configuration is a separate step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Credential references in config.</strong> Stack routes provider
      credential values through Phantom and records slot names in
      <code>.stack.toml</code>. Treat Terraform state as sensitive and secure
      its backend and access controls separately; Stack does not attest to
      what other tools write to disk or logs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>MCP-native.</strong> Stack's 19 MCP tools let an agent drive the
      supported lifecycle — recommend, apply, doctor, remove — from a
      Claude Code session. Terraform has no first-class MCP surface; you'd
      have to wrap the CLI yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Zero state server.</strong> No Terraform Cloud, no S3 backend,
      no DynamoDB lock table. <code>.stack.toml</code> gets checked in; Phantom
      holds the values. For a solo dev or a small team, that eliminates a
      whole category of operational overhead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>OAuth where supported.</strong> Some Stack adapters use browser
      authorization; others require an existing provider token. Terraform
      expects you to have created long-lived tokens in each provider's
      dashboard before <code>terraform apply</code> runs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where Terraform wins</h2>
  <p>Honest concessions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloud primitives.</strong> Stack does not create VPCs, subnets,
      security groups, IAM roles, RDS instances, GKE clusters, or Azure
      Function Apps. If you own cloud infrastructure, Terraform is the
      standard and Stack is not a replacement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Provider ecosystem.</strong> Terraform has 3,000+ providers on
      the registry, covering every major cloud, database, monitoring tool,
      DNS provider, VPN, and identity system. Stack ships 39 curated
      providers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>State and drift detection.</strong> <code>terraform plan</code>
      compares desired to actual state across thousands of resources; Stack
      only checks the presence and validity of declared SaaS services via{" "}
      <code>stack doctor</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Enterprise controls.</strong> Terraform Cloud has SOC2, SAML
      SSO, audit logs, policy-as-code via Sentinel, team RBAC, and private
      registries. Stack is pre-alpha; team-tier features are on the roadmap.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Multi-env and workspaces.</strong> Terraform has first-class
      support for dev/staging/prod environments with shared modules. Stack v0.1
      is single-environment-per-project.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Can I use both?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes, and it's the most common pattern for teams with any serious cloud
    footprint. Terraform owns the cloud account — VPCs, IAM, RDS, ECS,
    CloudFront — and Stack owns the SaaS layer: Supabase for data, Stripe
    for billing, Clerk for auth, PostHog for analytics, Sentry for errors.
    Hand-offs happen via environment variables: Terraform outputs feed into
    Stack config. <code>phantom exec -- terraform apply</code> delegates
    the Terraform command to Phantom; verify its environment and state
    handling separately.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're on OpenTofu (the Apache 2.0 fork of Terraform), the same
    pattern works — Stack doesn't care which CLI you use for the cloud side.
  </p>

  <h2>Migration and caveats</h2>
  <p>
    Stack is pre-alpha — these comparisons reflect v{PUBLISHED_VERSION} as of April 2026.
    We do not claim parity with Terraform's maturity, provider breadth, or
    enterprise tooling. If you already have a working Terraform monorepo,
    don't rip it out; add Stack for the SaaS services you're currently
    hand-wiring. For the provider catalog, see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/providers/">/docs/providers</a>. For agentic automation
    via MCP, see <a href="/docs/mcp/">/docs/mcp</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Can Stack replace Terraform?</h3>
  <p>
    Not for cloud infrastructure. Terraform remains the industry standard for
    provisioning AWS, GCP, Azure, and on-prem resources with a huge provider
    ecosystem and mature state management. Stack replaces the hand-written
    glue around SaaS services — Supabase, Stripe, Clerk — where Terraform
    providers are thin or non-existent.
  </p>

  <h3>Does Terraform have a Supabase or Stripe provider?</h3>
  <p>
    There is a community Supabase provider with limited coverage and a Stripe
    provider that mostly wraps the API surface. Stack supports Supabase project creation and Phantom credential routing.
    Its Stripe adapter verifies and stores an existing restricted key; setup
    behavior varies by provider.
  </p>

  <h3>Which should a two-person startup pick?</h3>
  <p>
    If you deploy to Vercel, Fly, or Railway and rent the rest (Supabase,
    Stripe, Clerk, PostHog), Stack can help connect supported SaaS services. If
    you run on AWS primitives — EC2, RDS, VPCs — you need Terraform for
    infra and can add Stack for the SaaS layer on top.
  </p>
</Docs>
